					<section class="major-section">
						<h2 class="section-title">🔥 Environmental Interactions</h2>
						<blockquote class="quote">"The arena is a monster, too."</blockquote>

						<p>Hunters can always attempt to:</p>
						<ul>
							<li>Drop trees</li>
							<li>Throw rocks</li>
							<li>Collapse weak terrain</li>
							<li>Flip logs</li>
							<li>Kick loose footing</li>
							<li>Set fires</li>
							<li>Trigger hazards</li>
						</ul>

						<h3>Universal Ruling</h3>
						<p>Skill check → result determines effect:</p>
						<ul>
							<li>Success: hazard triggers, monster makes save</li>
							<li>Fail: wasted action or partial hazard</li>
							<li>Crit fail: hazard backfires (smoke blinds, branch snaps, etc.)</li>
						</ul>
						<p>Use DC 10–16 based on danger & complexity.</p>
					</section>

					<section class="major-section">
						<h2 class="section-title">🌊 Underwater Combat</h2>
						<blockquote class="quote">"The water is not the enemy — but it has no interest in keeping you alive."</blockquote>

						<p>Fighting underwater is about pressure, movement, and oxygen, not a page of penalties.</p>
						<p>The following rules keep water combat dramatic but fun.</p>

						<h3>🫁 Breath & Survival</h3>
						<p>Breath determines how long a hunter can operate before the body starts shutting down. Keep it simple:</p>

						<h4>Breath Timer</h4>
						<p>Hunters can hold breath for: 1 + CON Modifier turns</p>
						<p>Surfacing resets the timer instantly.</p>

						<h4>Suffocating</h4>
						<p>At 0 breath:</p>
						<ul>
							<li>Lose 5 Stamina per turn</li>
							<li>If Stamina hits 0 → lose HP equal to your CON mod per turn</li>
						</ul>
						<p>Water should feel dangerous, not instant death.</p>

						<h3>🏊‍♂️ Movement in Water</h3>
						<p>Think of water as "slow 3D space."</p>

						<h4>Movement Rules</h4>
						<ul>
							<li>All movement costs +1 Stamina (unless water-adapted)</li>
							<li>Dashing costs 2x Stamina</li>
							<li>No jumping; upward/downward movement costs the same as horizontal</li>
							<li>Fights become more about position and currents than sprinting</li>
						</ul>

						<h4>3D Positioning (Simple Ruling)</h4>
						<p>A creature "above" you in the water has natural leverage:</p>
						<ul>
							<li>Gain advantage on shove/grapple</li>
							<li>You have disadvantage on escape attempts</li>
						</ul>

						<h3>⚔️ Combat Adjustments</h3>
						<p>Keep these light — water changes how weapons behave, not whether they work.</p>

						<h4>Weapon Logic</h4>
						<ul>
							<li>Slashing weapons: –1 damage (drag)</li>
							<li>Piercing weapons: +1 damage (ideal underwater)</li>
							<li>Blunt weapons: no change (water transmits force well)</li>
						</ul>

						<h4>Ranged Attacks</h4>
						<ul>
							<li>Bows, thrown weapons, and non-specialized guns: fail beyond 5 ft</li>
							<li>Specialized underwater gear bypasses this</li>
						</ul>

						<h4>Elemental & Fire Effects</h4>
						<ul>
							<li>Fire effects fail</li>
							<li>Lightning spreads (GM option: +1 die but reduced range)</li>
							<li>Ice partially forms — useful for walls, not projectiles</li>
							<li>Wind/air effects fail or behave strangely</li>
							<li>Water spells may gain +1 die or +1 DC depending on the feel of the magic</li>
						</ul>

						<p>Keep it GM-interpretive instead of hard-coded.</p>

						<h3>👁 Visibility</h3>
						<p>Water distorts sound and blurs sight.</p>

						<h4>Standard Visibility</h4>
						<ul>
							<li>Perception checks: +2 DC</li>
							<li>Murky or night water: heavily obscured</li>
						</ul>

						<h4>Exceptions</h4>
						<ul>
							<li>Bioluminescence</li>
							<li>Magical sight</li>
							<li>Sonar/echo hunters (certain beasts ignore visual issues entirely)</li>
						</ul>

						<h3>🌀 Passive Stamina Drain</h3>
						<p>Just being underwater is taxing.</p>
						<p>Each turn underwater costs +1 Stamina (breath control, pressure, drag).</p>
						<p>Adapted gear reduces or removes this.</p>
						<p>This creates time pressure without punishment.</p>

						<div class="callout"><strong>Example:</strong> Underwater 3D Chase</div>
						<p>The party is ambushed by an Apex Tide Serpent while diving for pearls. The fight starts in open water. The serpent coils below them, gaining the above position bonus (advantage on shoves). A hunter with a harpoon swims downward to flank. Each 10‑ft move costs +1 Stamina; a Dash would cost 2. The hunter spends 3 Stamina to reach the serpent's side. She uses a piercing harpoon (+1 damage underwater) and hits. On the serpent's turn, it attempts to grapple her; because of its higher position, she has disadvantage on her escape attempt. Another hunter tosses a net from above, forcing the serpent towards the seabed, where its mobility is reduced. This vignette shows how 3D movement, stamina drain, and position bonuses interplay without a grid.</p>

						<h3>🔥 Tactical Guidelines (GM Tools)</h3>
						<p>When players try to land or expose an aquatic creature, always reward the attempt.</p>
						<p>Underwater monsters gain their strength from mobility, depth, and water pressure.</p>
						<p>Anything that disrupts those — nets, shallow water, collisions, currents, debris — should knock them into a weaker phase or remove one of their advantages for a short time.</p>
						<p>Let clever plans shift the battle's tempo. This is the heart of the hunt.</p>
					</section>
